  "captions": {
    "01_primary_contrasts": "**Instruction contrasts.** All 24 planned contrasts are retained. Colored points\nare complete-block means, horizontal lines are the archived 95% simultaneous\nBonferroni t intervals for the 24-contrast family, and faint dots are individual\ncomplete paired blocks. Each block averages the same four fixed models before\nsubtracting same-seed US-English. The top three rows change country framing;\nthe bottom three change language while retaining US framing. Most rows have\neight blocks; the three exceptions are labeled. Global Japanese has five and\ntherefore no interval or primary test. Global uses density; the other panels\nuse clustering and share an axis range. Zero denotes no difference, not\nequivalence. Assumptions and missingness limits remain unchanged. Iterative US-Hindi passes the adjusted threshold in only 3 of 8 omission refits; these are overlapping sensitivity checks.",
    "02_primary_robustness": "**Sensitivity of iterative US-Hindi.** Left: all eight paired block differences.\nRight: means after omitting the indicated block; blocks 1-8 correspond to seeds\n44000-44007. The dashed line is the full-data mean, -0.045279. Filled refit\nmarkers pass the full-family Holm threshold, open markers do not (3/8 pass).\nNo refit intervals are shown. Overlapping leave-one-out fits are not independent\nreplications, and 3/8 is not a probability of replication. The separate exact\nsign-flip diagnostic has adjusted p=0.1875. This selected sensitivity view is\nnot a new confirmatory analysis or a statement about Hindi speakers.",
    "03_model_profiles": "**Fixed-model endpoint means.** Colors identify methods and shapes identify\nthe four fixed model configurations consistently. Each point averages valid,\ndefined endpoints in one eight-attempt cell. The US-English baseline is above\nthe separator. No confidence intervals or model rankings are implied. Global\nuses density, other methods clustering; their quantities are not interchangeable.\nDefined counts below eight are global Japanese for the three non-GPT-4.1\nconfigurations (7 each), global Portuguese/gpt-5.6-luna (6), and iterative\nJapanese/gpt-4.1 (7). Exact counts remain in descriptive_metrics.csv. Axes show the range of observed means, not the full theoretical domain. All three clustering panels use the same limits.",
    "04_coverage_missingness": "**Failures and missingness.** Left: all 896 original attempts, with six failures\nhatched rather than erased. All 12 valid empty graphs remain valid outcomes.\nRight: all three contrasts with fewer than eight complete blocks. Brown spans\nallow unavailable endpoints anywhere in [0,1]; these are identification bounds,\nNOT confidence intervals. Dots are complete-block means. The other 21 contrasts\nhave complete data and collapsed missingness bounds, not zero uncertainty.\nGlobal bounds are density differences and iterative bounds clustering differences.",
    "05_resource_use": "**Compute is unequal.** Every point is one fixed model/method group (56 attempts),\nincluding failed attempts. Received-cost estimates exclude unallocated retained\nreservations and are not invoices. Serial provider hours are not wall runtime.\nThe original resource totals are unchanged; no efficiency winner is inferred.",
    "06_gnm_context": "**Generated graphs versus matched random-edge references.** Faint dots show\nobserved clustering minus the mean of eight same-node, same-edge G(n,m) draws\nfor each valid, defined primary graph. Larger shapes show model/method means.\nAll seven instructions are pooled without new reweighting, as in the archived\nprimary figure. Zero is equality to that synthetic mean, not a realism threshold.\nNo confidence intervals or new tests. This is not the held-out transfer ranking.",
    "07_legacy_comparison": "Circles show the median and lines the middle half of valid, defined primary outcomes within each procedure. These are distribution summaries, not confidence intervals. Diamonds are individual development records; size and tie definitions differ, and the Tailor waves share workers.",
    "08_heldout_distances": "Lower W1 means closer on that metric alone. First average the four fixed-model distances, or the eight classical simulation-bank distances, within each record. Blue points average the ten Facebook records equally; bars show the smallest and largest record values, NOT confidence intervals. Orange diamonds are one school record. The same people can appear in more than one Facebook record. LLM scores weight all seven instructions equally among valid outcomes; failures are not zeros. These are not country-matched populations. Efficiency reflects fragmentation as well as path length. Largest-component results are in appendix 13.",
    "09_heldout_distributions": "Height is the fraction of networks at or below the value. Compare each generated curve with both observed sources: Facebook (solid black, equal record weights) and school (dashed black). Candidates appear only once per metric: top panels mix the four fixed models equally within a procedure, retaining equal instruction weights; lower panels pool eight equal-size banks per baseline. The density axis covers all observed values. Pooled distributions illustrate shape; distance between these curves is NOT the cohort-wise score in figure 08.",
    "10_sampling_effect": "**Sampling changes the reference.** Open circles show the full observable\nroster; diamonds show the average over 32 induced 50-node draws. Light spans\nare minima/maxima of those draws, NOT CIs. Lines join full and sampled means\nfor the same record. The observable school roster excludes unrecoverable\nnonresponders; Facebook cohorts and within-cohort samples overlap.",
    "11_conditional_controls": "Each of the 32 target graphs contributes one residual: observed coefficient minus the mean of its eight matched controls. Dots average those 32 differences; lines span their actual minimum to maximum, not confidence intervals. These are target-informed controls, not competitors. Edge count fixes density, and the degree sequence fixes Gini: zero residuals there are identities. Finite swaps do not establish uniform mixing; a positive mean is not a significance test.",
    "12_cohort_sensitivity": "Each row is one observed record. Panels share one clustering W1 scale. Four fixed models per LLM procedure remain separate, and the last panel shows each classical baseline averaged over its eight banks. These 19 candidates show variability hidden by procedure means. Rows are not independent population replicates.",
    "13_appendix_lcc": "Appendix: largest-component fraction, with the same record-level W1 averaging and descriptive ranges as figure 08. 447/448 ER graphs and 448/448 BA and WS graphs are connected. Distances can nearly coincide without being exactly identical; this metric is retained, not deleted."
  },
